//! The agent skill (`plied skill`, FORMAT.md §11). Rendered by the binary from its own
//! version and command inventory, so a copy an agent installs cannot drift from the plied
//! it was generated by. The prose is the part that cannot be derived: the working habits
//! that `check` cannot teach because getting them wrong fails silently or much later.

/// Render the skill. `commands` is one `` - `plied …` — about `` line per (sub)command,
/// built by the binary from its own parser definition.
pub fn render(version: &str, commands: &[String]) -> String {
    let commands = commands.join("\n");
    let exits = (0..=6)
        .map(|c| format!("- `{c}` {}", crate::error::exit_name(c)))
        .collect::<Vec<_>>()
        .join("\n");
    format!(
        r#"---
name: plied
description: Work in a project that keeps its record in plied (a plied.toml and a plied/ directory). Use for reading or changing threads, commitments and questions, messaging whoever is on a node, reviewing commitments before an action they govern, and keeping `plied check` clean.
---

# plied {version}

Generated by `plied skill` from plied {version}. Do not edit a copy of this
file: regenerate it (`plied skill > SKILL.md`) whenever plied is upgraded, so
the guidance cannot drift from the installed binary.

plied is a work record that lives in git: threads (nodes), commitments made
along the way, questions waiting on authority, and messages to whoever is on a
node. Records are plain files, but change them through `plied`: each write is
checked, appends a history line, and names the revision it was made against.

## Orient

```sh
export PLIED_AGENT=your-name          # or pass --as on every command
export PLIED_SESSION="$(date -u +%Y%m%dT%H%M%SZ)-$$"  # unique to this shell session
plied context --json                 # selected store, blockers, identity and vocabularies
plied schema thread --json           # fields, required conditions and example records
```

Choose the actual node named in your task; use `frontier`/`tree` if no node
was supplied. Below, `NODE` means that actual ID, never an assumed first ID.
Set it in your shell before running the sequence. Check `context`'s actor
and action vocabularies before assigning either; agent identity and the
scheduled actor are separate. Missing configuration: run `plied init` at
the selected canonical checkout, then inspect `context` again.

```sh
plied --json check                    # baseline: know the errors you did not make
                                      # (failing check = exit 1, report still in result.diagnostics)
plied --json frontier                 # queues by actor, unscheduled threads, open questions, dates
plied --json tree                     # the whole work tree
plied show "$NODE" --json             # read text and its revision together; retain both
plied messages "$NODE"               # earlier conversation does not retarget to a new claimant
plied review "$NODE"                 # read every applicable commitment before acting
plied claim "$NODE"                  # presence; also lists the commitments that apply
plied inbox --peek                    # --peek first: plain `inbox` marks read
```

Claims expire after the configured lease. Without the Claude hook, renew by
running `claim` again with the same agent/session before expiry. Use the
revision you read with the text for your next checked write; the detailed
show → --expect cycle follows. Before handing off, record what remains and
where, then release only this session (`release --this-session` requires a
nonempty session). New sessions get new IDs, even for the same agent.

Task map: **Orient** (`context`, `schema`, `show`, `messages`); **Update**
(`new`, `set`, `note`, `replace`); **Review** (`review`, `dispose`, `check`);
**Communicate** (`claim`, `send`, `inbox`, `release`); **Recover** (`show`
after exit 3, `recover` for interrupted writes, `index` for a changed policy).
`--help` groups commands by task; `COMMAND --help` gives exact arguments.

The frontier is only as true as `actor` and `next`. When you take up a
thread, set them (`actor=<you> next=1`; `actor` must be one of
`checks.actors` when the project sets that) in the same write as your first
note on it; when you set it down, clear `next` or change its status, so the
next session sees what is really queued. When you park work outside the
record (a branch, a stash, scratch files, a run left going), note on the
node what it is and where, at the time you park it, not at hand-off: a
session can end without handing off, and a lease that lapses leaves no
trace. Every write regenerates the view: there is no need to run
`plied view` after writing. If `check` reports the view out of date,
someone edited records by hand or a merge brought them in, so run
`plied view` once and read what its diff changed.

## Writes: the show → --expect cycle

Every write names the revision it was made against: the revision of the
text you read, taken in the same command that showed you that text.

1. `plied show T001` prints `rev: 24c12b2de96d` above the record, and
   `--expect` takes that prefix as it is (any 8 or more leading hex digits),
   so there is nothing to parse. A script reads `result.rev` from
   `plied --json show T001` instead. Never fetch a rev separately at write
   time: if the record changed after you read it, that rev would let your
   write through unchecked.
2. `plied set T001 actor=bob --expect <rev> --why "…"` → on success it prints
   the new revision (`updated T001 (rev …)`; under `--json`, `result.rev`, and
   `new` also returns `result.id`).
3. **Re-read the target's rev after every call.** In a batch of writes, chain
   each call's new rev into the next call on the same record, or `show` it
   again. Never reuse a rev across two writes to the same record, and
   never write the same record in parallel.

Exit 3 is a conflict: the record moved on. `show` it again, reconcile with
the current text (the error says how it changed), and retry — there is no
force flag and no silent overwrite. Exit 4 means the change would add
validation errors; they are in `error.detail.diagnostics`.

Assignments work on `new` and `set`: `key=value` sets, `key=` clears,
`key+=item` / `key-=item` edit a list, dotted keys nest
(`approval.state=approved`), and a list of ids is set in flow form, quoted
for the shell — `plied new commitment --title "…" 'nodes=[T001, T002]'`. A
flow list splits at every unquoted comma, so give prose items (`options`) one
`+=` each: `'options+=keep A, then B' 'options+=drop it'`. Long values come
from `--set-file key=FILE` (or `--body FILE` on `new`).

## Commitments: quote, attach, review

- `wording` is a **normalised quotation of `made`**: the exact words of the
  record it cites, after normalisation of both sides (whitespace collapsed,
  curly quotes straightened, `*` and backticks removed — `_` is not —,
  hyphenated line breaks joined). It is matched against the record's whole
  file, whatever locator `made` carries. Case changes and paraphrases fail. Quote non-contiguous parts by joining
  them with ` … ` (or `...`); the parts must appear in order. If the words
  are not in the record yet, write the record first — never approximate.
- Attach with `nodes=[T001, T002]`, or set `applies: subtree` / `project`.
  A `parent` link alone never carries an obligation.
- A resolved state (`fulfilled`, `waived`, `missed`, `not-triggered`,
  `superseded`) needs `resolved_by`. A **waived** or **superseded**
  commitment is authority-checked: `resolved_by` must name a linked decision
  record (not a plied record) whose authority is accepted and whose scope
  includes the commitment's own id. "Cannot be verified" is a warning, never
  a pass.
- When a decision replaces a commitment with another (new timing, new
  wording), record the new one first, then set the old one
  `state=superseded superseded_by=<new> resolved_by=<decision>` in one write.
  Not `waived`: a waiver says the obligation is gone, and here it lives on.
  Attach the new one wherever the old one applied (`check` warns when it does
  not). Write one commitment per clause: when one clause of a commitment is
  kept and another replaced, it is superseded, and the `--why` says which
  clause was kept.
- `governs=[report]` says a commitment governs only actions of those kinds
  (from `checks.action_kinds`), so the gate leaves it out of a `run`. Set it
  from the wording only: a rule about how readings are reported governs
  `report`, while "before any run" governs `run`. When the wording does not
  confine itself to a kind, leave `governs` unset. A wrong `governs` opens a
  gate silently, so `review` still lists the commitments it leaves out: read
  them.
- Before any action a commitment could govern (a run, a freeze, closing
  work), gate it:
  `plied review T001 --action run:fit-20261024 --gate` exits 6 until every
  applicable commitment has a disposition **for that exact action** and the
  latest one is not `blocks`. Record yours with `plied dispose P01 --action
  run:fit-20261024 --disposition satisfied --because "…" --expect <rev>`
  (re-read P01's rev after every dispose, as for any write). A passing gate
  means each commitment was reviewed for this action — not that it is
  fulfilled.
- A commitment's wording decides what it covers, not your sense of how big
  the action is. One that says "before any run of X" covers every run of X:
  a smoke, a toy run or a test that runs the governed code counts. When in
  doubt, gate it.
- Before carrying out a step of a plan an earlier session wrote, run
  `plied review <node>` (no `--gate`) and read the list. A step written down
  earlier is not a step reviewed: commitments may have been made since, and
  the plan may never have been gated.
- `--gate` requires at least one node and `--action`; omitting a node list is
  a usage error (exit 2). A missing or invalid latest disposition holds the gate.
- The gate examines only the nodes you name. Name every node the action
  depends on or produces results for (`plied review T001 T004 T009 …`), not
  only the one you are on: a commitment attached to a node you left out
  never comes up. Run it before the action, and use a fresh action name
  (`run:fit-<date>`) each time, so an old review never stands in for a new
  run. A `because` says what you checked, not that you checked.

### Controls before citing a null result

When an experimental plan requires a positive control or manipulation check,
record that requirement as a commitment before the experiment. For example,
if D001 says "Before citing a null, recover the known positive and verify the
delivered dose", quote it exactly:

```sh
plied new commitment --title "Controls before citing a null" made=D001 \
  'wording=Before citing a null, recover the known positive and verify the delivered dose' \
  'nodes=[T001]'
plied review T001 --action cite-null:run-01 --gate
```

If `checks.action_kinds` is configured, it must include `cite-null`. Leave
`governs` unset unless the plan confines the requirement to particular action
kinds. Use a fresh action name for each proposed citation. Read the control
results against the plan's criteria, then `show` the commitment and record a
disposition with that revision. For example, if the known positive was missed:

```sh
plied show P01
plied dispose P01 --action cite-null:run-01 --disposition blocks \
  --because "The detector missed the known positive; this null cannot support the claim" \
  --ref E002 --expect <rev>
plied review T001 --action cite-null:run-01 --gate
```

Use the id returned by `new` in place of P01, and the actual results record in
place of E002. Record `satisfied` only after judging the evidence against the
criteria; a missing or failed control calls for `blocks` while the requirement
remains unmet. The gate checks that the commitment was reviewed for this
action and no latest disposition is `blocks`, not that the control passed.
It does not evaluate the measurements or establish that the reviewed evidence
is unchanged. A null from a tool that missed its known positive is not made
informative by a passing gate.

## Scan triage: commitment, scan, or neither

`plied check` warns `commitment?` when a linked record from a
`scan_commitments` source contains a conditional or deferral that no
commitment records. Read the record first (`plied show D123`, and keep the
`rev:` it prints), then decide:

- **Record a commitment** when the text obliges a future check or act: a
  conditional promise ("if X, then Y"), a precondition ("before Z, do W"),
  a deferral to a trigger or a date — anything a reviewer would have to
  disposition before some action. Create it with the verbatim `wording`,
  `made=<that record>`, attached to the nodes it governs.
- **Record a scan** when you have read the whole record at that revision
  and it holds no such obligation:
  `plied scanned D123 --rev <its rev> --on T001 --because "read in full: no conditional or deferral" --expect <T001's rev>`
  (the `--expect` is the *thread's* rev — re-read it first). A scan covers
  only the revision read; when the record changes, the warning returns and
  you scan again.
- **Do neither** when you have not read it in full or are unsure. Leave the
  warning standing. Scanning to silence a warning, or minting a commitment
  from a keyword hit alone, both corrupt the record.

## Stale warnings: note each node's review

A node goes **stale** when a dated linked record, newer than the node's last
history line, mentions the node or anything it links to, except `parent` and
a commitment's `nodes` attachment. A record about a thread does not by that
attachment alone make all its commitments stale. Naming a commitment directly,
or something in its `made` or `links`, still counts; a question's `nodes`
still counts too. One wide-citing entry can therefore make several nodes
stale at once, and a warning does not clear itself.
A resolved record (closed thread, fulfilled or waived commitment, answered
question) goes stale only when the entry names it directly. Then the
question is whether the entry reopens it: say which in the note, and if it
does, reopen it in the same write.

`check` reports nodes the entry **names** first (subject `named` under
`--json`), then nodes it reaches only **through their links** (`links`). The
named ones are the likelier to need a change, so start there, but every
warning still gets its review.

For each stale warning: read the new entry, decide whether it changes the
node, then **note each node's review** so the record shows it happened (or
make the change it calls for instead, and note that). Read the entry once,
then take its nodes one by one: `show` each, which gives you its rev with
its text. Never batch-note nodes you have not read the entry against — the
note is the evidence the review happened.

Twenty stale nodes take twenty reads. In a script, the rev you pass is
the one from the call whose text you judged:

```sh
plied --json show T001    # read result.text against the entry; keep result.rev
plied note T001 "reviewed D140: …" --expect <that result.rev>
```

A loop that fetches revs just before writing (from `list`, or from a fresh
`show` whose text nobody read) defeats the check: the rev no longer shows
that the note was written against the current text. Write each note from
that node's own text; one note pasted across a group is a rubber stamp.

Say in the note what the entry touches and why the node is or is not
affected, so a later reader can check your reasoning:
`plied note T001 "reviewed D140: it changes the outlier rule; T001 only formats the tables, so it is unaffected" --expect <rev>`.
"reviewed D140: no change" records that you looked, not what you saw, and a
string of them is a rubber stamp.

The check counts any id-shaped text as a mention. When an entry uses an id
as a name for something else (a simulation set called "P20" is not
commitment P20), note exactly that on the node: `"D146 names a simulation
set P20; it does not cite commitment P20"`. Never edit a linked record to
quiet a warning: linked records belong to their own system, and decisions
belong to whoever made them.

## Messages and presence

`claim` is an expiring lease, not a lock or a proof of life. Message the
node, not the person: `plied send T001 "…" --id <your-unique-id>` reaches
whoever is on it now plus subscribers; recipients are fixed at send time,
and retrying with the same `--id` is recorded once. `inbox` marks messages
read (`--peek` does not); `ack` is a receipt, never an approval.

## Closing, questions, authority

Closing records what happened — it is not success, and it approves nothing
downstream. A closed thread needs `outcome.quote` quoted (as for `wording`)
from `outcome.ref`, plus `carries` for problems it leaves open. Set them in
the same write as the status — `status=closed` alone is refused (exit 4):
`plied set T001 status=closed 'outcome.quote=…' outcome.ref=D150 --expect <rev> --why "…"`. A question is
answered only by `answered_by` naming a record with the authority and scope
for it, just as `approval.by` is checked the same way. An answer you hear in
conversation is not yet an answer: get it written into a decision record the
project accepts, in the decider's words, then set `answered_by` to that
record. If you have no way to record it (a side conversation without tools),
say so and ask for it to be sent where it can be.

## Reusing a result: check its scope

Before using an earlier outcome to settle a new task, read the cited evidence
and record a short comparison on the new task:

- **Mapping:** write the proposed object in the earlier result's notation.
- **Assumptions:** say which necessary premises still hold, which changed,
  and which remain unknown. A renamed object or a narrower subset may still
  be covered; a changed parameter does not by itself escape the old result.
- **Consequence:** say what the comparison supports: reuse the result,
  investigate the changed case, or resolve a specific uncertainty. Name the
  evidence that supports this judgment.

A valid old result stays valid within its scope. Keep its closed outcome
when opening a distinct task outside that scope. A new hypothesis does not
by itself withdraw the old conclusion or show the new attempt will succeed.
When the old conclusion or its rationale is defective, preserve the original
claim and a linked correction explaining the reason and scope before changing
the thread; reopening currently clears `outcome`, so retain it in that linked
record first. A reference to a corrected result does not make every dependent
conclusion invalid automatically.

Use `uses` and `waiting_on` for actual dependencies: they carry applicable
commitments, not a proof that a scientific conclusion transfers. Use a prose
reference or `links` for a prior thread consulted only for comparison. Linking
a commitment attaches it. Keep research preferences such as "avoid another
unchanged sweep by default" in prose unless they are actual obligations.
A genuine requirement to check hypotheses can be recorded as a commitment;
the gate checks its recorded review, not the correctness of the judgment.

For a consequential result, surface its **object, necessary assumptions,
bounded conclusion, evidence and reconsideration condition** in the thread
body at the decision, rather than waiting until hand-off. These are prose,
not new fields. `revisit` is prose too: it does not reopen work automatically.
Search results are candidates to inspect; a high similarity score does not
establish applicability, and no search hits do not establish that work is new.

## Optional thread summaries

When a thread is difficult to resume or browse, add a short `## Summary`
section to its body. Say what it concerns, what is established and under which
conditions, what remains unresolved or should happen next, and where the
evidence lives. Keep essential qualifications even when shortening it.

Use `new --body FILE` for a new thread. For an existing thread, read its full
record and revision with `show`, preserve its frontmatter and the rest of the
file (including History), and use
`replace` with that revision. `note` appends to History; a Summary heading
inside History is not the current synopsis. Use `replace --help` for the
arguments. Update a summary when a substantive result or next decision makes
it misleading; there is no need to add one or rewrite it on every edit.

Orientation and retrieval may show it as `body_summary`, with the source
record's revision. This is a read-only extraction, not a frontmatter field.
Previews can be shortened and summaries can be outdated: read the full
record, source evidence and exact commitment wording before acting. A preview
and its revision do not stand in for reading the text of a checked write.
Summaries do not establish accuracy, freshness, authority or approval, and
never change a gate or a status. No automatic summary generation is implied.

## Before you hand off

For each node you claimed, note what is unfinished and where it is (a
branch, a stash, scratch files, a run still going), and set `next` or
`status` to match. Then run `plied check`. Hand off with 0 errors and 0
warnings, or name every warning left standing and why (an unscanned
record, a node whose review you did not note). Do not claim a clean check
you did not run in this session. Then `plied release --this-session` so
your claims stop showing you as on the node (they expire on their own, but
later).

## Exit codes

{exits}

Meanings: 1 error or failed check · 2 usage · 3 conflict (the record moved
on since `--expect`) · 4 the change would add validation errors · 5 blocked
(branch, merge state, version pin, guard refusal) · 6 review gate.

Under `--json`, parse stdout even on nonzero exits. A failed verdict has
`result` (for example `result.diagnostics` or `result.gate.reasons`); a
command failure has `error` (a conflict or refused write may have
`error.detail`). Exit 3: re-read text, reconcile and retry. Exit 4: inspect
the added diagnostics and correct the proposed change. Exit 5: inspect
`context` and the pending intent; resolve branch/version blockers before
`recover`. Exit 6: read the applicable commitments and record an honest
disposition for that exact action. Never treat a nonzero exit as missing JSON.

## Commands (generated from this binary)

{commands}
"#
    )
}

